<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Description de l'Égypte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Description de l'Égypte is a large illustrated survey of ancient and modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and its natural history, compiled by the scholars who accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s expedition of 1798–1801. Published by the French government in many volumes between 1809 and 1829, its plates of temples, monuments, plants and daily life greatly stimulated European interest in Egypt and helped lay the foundations of Egyptology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/description-de-legypte.jpg" alt="Description de l'Égypte" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Description_de_l%C2%B4Egypte_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>About 160 civilian scholars and scientists, the savants, many of them drawn from the Institut de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, accompanied the expedition and formed the Commission des Sciences et Arts d'Égypte. In late August 1798 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institut-degypte">Institut d'Égypte</a> was founded on the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> with Gaspard Monge as president. It had a library, laboratories and a workshop that built instruments to replace those lost when the French fleet was sunk at Aboukir Bay. In November 1798 Joseph Fourier was entrusted with uniting the reports of the different disciplines for later publication.</p><p>When the army left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1801 the savants took their notes, drawings and small collections with them past the British. In February 1802, at the instigation of the interior minister Jean Antoine Chaptal, a commission was set up to prepare the work, which was issued piece by piece as plates or texts on a subject were ready. The first test volumes of engravings were presented to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in January 1808; later volumes came out by order of the king and the last by order of the government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first edition usually has nine volumes of text, a volume describing the plates and ten volumes of plates, with two more in the very large Mammut format, about 1 m by 0.81 m, and an atlas: twenty-three volumes in all. Its 894 plates were made from over 3,000 drawings, and about 2,000 artists and technicians, among them 400 engravers, worked on it. The maps of the Carte de l'Égypte were the first triangulation-based map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. A cheaper second edition, published by Charles Louis Fleury Panckoucke, usually runs to thirty-seven volumes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Carte de l'Égypte was the basis of most maps of the region for much of the 19th century and was used in planning a canal through the Isthmus of Suez. Critics have read the work as an instrument of empire: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-said">Edward Said</a> called it &quot;that great collective appropriation of one country by another&quot;, and Anne Godlewska argued that writing the maps in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet was a deliberate choice. On 17 December 2011 a set of the first edition survived a fire at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institut-degypte">Institut d'Égypte</a>, contrary to reports that the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> had burned; most of the manuscript material is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Description de l'Égypte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French expedition to Egypt of 1798–1801 brought, besides an army, a printing press and a commission of scholars whose work became the <b>Description de l’Égypte</b>; al-Jabarti recorded both his curiosity and his disdain.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Bonaparte brought scholars who founded the Institut d'Égypte and compiled the <b>Description de l'Égypte</b>; the Rosetta Stone was found in 1799.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-occupation-of-egypt">French occupation of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
